WEBVTT

STYLE
::cue {
  font-family: Arial;
  font-size: 6.94vh;
  color: rgba(255,255,255,1);
  background-color: rgba(0,0,0,0.6);
}

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:06.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can knit in circles using short rows,
and the bonus is a pinwheel shape

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
that's formed automatically.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:15.653 line:95% align:center position:50%
Use the Knit it Now circle calculator tool
for the perfect math to create your

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:21.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
circular shawls, blankets, pillows,
dishrags or coasters, whatever.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let's walk through the process
and look at some variations.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:31.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm sure you can imagine a lot
of things you can do with circles.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're going to be knitting
the circle in one piece.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:36.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
Only one seam.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.413 line:95% align:center position:50%
Change colors or not.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:40.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's your choice.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
Start with waste yarn and ravel cord.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:47.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
Knit one row.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now this is going to make
the final seaming easier.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
Although I normally start knitting
with the carriage on the right,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.453 line:95% align:center position:50%
for this I started with the carriage
on the left and I knit that first

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
row from left to right.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Pull all of your needles into hold
position and set your carriage to hold.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
Following your pattern instructions
for your gauge and your size,

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
put the first set of needles
into working position.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:19.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
And knit across.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
Be sure and wrap that first needle
to prevent holes and knit back.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.173 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now this is Short Rowing 101.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
Nothing fancy here.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:35.493 line:95% align:center position:50%
Continue putting groups of needles in work
as directed and wrapping to prevent holes

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
until all the needles
are in working position.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
End with the carriage on the right
and your first wedge is complete.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:08.293 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now you can change colors— or not—
and start the next wedge by putting

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
all of the needles into hold again.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
If you're changing colors, you're going
to have a bunch of ends to work in.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:21.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Try weaving the ends over and under
the needles and work them in as you knit.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.973 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now this can get a little bit tricky when
working short rows and wrapping

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
the needles, but it really saves time.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Short row to complete the second wedge.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:40.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
The beauty of this is No
seams between the wedges.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Continue knitting wedges as
directed in your instructions.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:14.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
And when they're complete,
scrap off with a contrasting color yarn.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
Use Kitchener stitch to create a seam.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:23.173 line:95% align:center position:50%
And I challenge anyone to see where you
started and where you

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:24.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
stopped your pinwheel.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:31.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
In this example, stripes were created
by changing colors every other row.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:35.533 line:95% align:center position:50%
Hmm, this one makes me want to try this
technique with a variegated

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
yarn with long color repeats.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:43.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
What happens when you don't
wrap when you short row?

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:44.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
You get holes!

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
But maybe eyelets are what you want.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can thread ribbon or i-cord through
the eyelets for a decorative effect.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.133 line:95% align:center position:50%
Speaking of finishing,
The fastest way to work all the way around

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:02.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
the outside of that big
circle is with crochet.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:08.013 line:95% align:center position:50%
Single crochet alone or a round of single
crochet plus a round

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:09.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
of crab stitch looks great.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:15.053 line:95% align:center position:50%
If you are crochet impaired,
try picking up the edge

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
of the circle and knitting I-cord.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:18.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
Beautiful.

00:04:19.567 --> 00:04:23.813 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can use these techniques to finish
and join the fronts and backs

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
of a pillow as well.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
I hope you're inspired
by circles to try your own.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:38.213 line:95% align:center position:50%
With the calculator, you can use any yarn,
any machine or hand knitting

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
needles, and any gauge.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:45.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
Hmm, what about adding some
stitch patterns or lace?

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
Lots of possibilities with circles.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:50.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Happy knitting!

